(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-vignettes"]={"title":"Dermatology II Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has erythema nodosum, bilateral ankle arthritis and hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph. Which is the most likely underlying diagnosis?","choices":["Primary tuberculosis with mediastinal involvement","Streptococcal infection with post-infectious reaction","Sarcoidosis presenting as Löfgren syndrome","Inflammatory bowel disease, most likely Crohn's disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it has the best prognosis of all sarcoidosis presentations, with over 90% spontaneous remission. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man reports excessive sweating of both palms and soles since early adolescence, enough to smudge his handwriting. It never occurs while he is asleep. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary focal hyperhidrosis","Secondary generalised hyperhidrosis","Frey syndrome producing gustatory sweating","Autonomic neuropathy from undiagnosed diabetes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bilateral, focal, adolescent onset and absent during sleep are the defining features. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has several uniformly tan, well-demarcated macules 5 to 12 mm across on the dorsal hands and forearms, with no scale or induration. Dermoscopy shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Solar lentigo","Lentigo maligna","Seborrheic keratosis","Pigmented actinic keratosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — uniform pigment on chronically exposed skin with those dermoscopic features. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops itchy 3 mm red papules on her chest and forearms hours after the first sunny weekend of spring. The same thing happened last April and settled by midsummer. Her face is spared. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Solar urticaria of the chest and forearms","Erythropoietic protoporphyria of adult onset","Polymorphous light eruption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — spring onset, sparing of chronically exposed skin, and summer hardening are all characteristic. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man returns from a beach holiday with painful blisters followed by odd streaked brown marks on both forearms. He had been squeezing limes into drinks in the sun. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Berloque dermatitis from bergapten in a fragrance product","Chronic actinic dermatitis of the forearms and dorsal hands","Allergic contact dermatitis to an ingredient in his sunscreen","Phytophotodermatitis from furanocoumarins in lime juice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — linear streaked hyperpigmentation after lime plus sun is the classic presentation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man reports months of intensely itchy, burning bumps on his elbows, knees and buttocks. Examination shows grouped papules and excoriations symmetrically distributed, with few intact vesicles. He has no gastrointestinal complaints. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis of adult onset","Linear immunoglobulin A bullous dermatosis","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Scabies infestation of the trunk and extremities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the symmetric extensor distribution with burning that precedes lesions, and gastrointestinal symptoms may be absent despite villous atrophy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has itchy wheals that persist for two to three days and fade leaving brownish discoloration. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Skin biopsy, since lesions persisting beyond twenty-four hours suggest urticarial vasculitis","Increase the second-generation antihistamine to four times the standard licensed daily dose","Measure complement C4 and C1-esterase inhibitor level and function","Begin omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the persistence and residual bruising are what take this out of simple urticaria. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman wants treatment for photoaging of her face. Which topical agent is the only one approved by the Food and Drug Administration for this indication?","choices":["Niacinamide applied twice daily","Tretinoin at 0.025 to 0.1%","Vitamin C at 10 to 20%","Hydroquinone at 2 to 4%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it stimulates collagen and inhibits matrix metalloproteinases, over six to twelve months. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman spent six hours at the beach. Eight hours later her shoulders are red, hot and tender, without blisters. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["A short course of oral corticosteroids tapered over the following week","Cold water immersion with ice packs applied directly to the erythema","Topical silver sulfadiazine applied twice daily under an occlusive layer","Cool compresses, oral hydration and an early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — early nonsteroidals reduce the prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had chronic spontaneous urticaria for eight months and asks whether it will ever settle. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Complete avoidance of all common food allergens will resolve the condition","About half of chronic urticaria resolves spontaneously within a year, and over half of cases have no identifiable trigger","A specific environmental or dietary trigger can be identified in almost every case if enough testing is done","The condition is lifelong once it has persisted beyond six weeks in duration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this reframes the search for a cause that often does not exist. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent episodes of lip and periorbital swelling without any wheals. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Serum tryptase with a search for Darier's sign on examination","Autologous serum skin test for autoimmune chronic urticaria","Thyroid-stimulating hormone with anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Complement C4 with C1-esterase inhibitor level and function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — angio-oedema without wheals should raise hereditary angio-oedema. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with newly confirmed dermatitis herpetiformis is about to start dapsone. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status before the first dose","Check thyroid-stimulating hormone and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Arrange small bowel biopsy before any treatment is commenced","Perform HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 typing to confirm susceptibility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, then monitor the blood count for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is admitted with Stevens-Johnson syndrome six days after starting trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Which is the single most important initial action?","choices":["Begin high-dose systemic corticosteroids and continue for several weeks","Apply silver sulfadiazine dressings to the areas of detached epidermis","Discontinue the suspected drug and every other non-essential medication","Begin intravenous immunoglobulin at one gram per kilogram for three days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival, and each day of delay worsens it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man reports new generalised sweating including at night, with episodes of headache and palpitations, and his blood pressure is 178/104. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Minor's starch-iodine test to map the distribution of sweating","Gravimetric measurement of axillary sweat output over five minutes","Photopatch testing to a standard series of photoallergens","Twenty-four hour urine metanephrines and catecholamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — episodic sweating with headache, palpitations and hypertension points to phaeochromocytoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has her fourth episode of target lesions on the hands in eighteen months. Each episode has followed an oral herpes outbreak. Which is the most appropriate long-term management?","choices":["Dapsone titrated to response with regular blood count monitoring","Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily taken continuously","Acyclovir 400 mg five times daily during each acute episode only","Suppressive acyclovir 400 mg twice daily or valacyclovir 500 mg daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — recurrent disease is strongly associated with recurrent herpes simplex, and suppression is the answer. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient with pyoderma gangrenosum asks why the wound clinic will not debride the ulcer as they would for other wounds. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Debridement is deferred only until systemic immunosuppression has taken effect","The ulcer worsens with trauma, a phenomenon called pathergy, so debridement makes it larger","The ulcer is infected and debridement would spread bacteria into the bloodstream","The ulcer will heal faster if left completely undisturbed by any dressing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the single most consequential management point in the condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 55-year-old kidney transplant recipient asks why she is being seen so often for skin checks. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Frequent review is a routine protocol requirement rather than a clinical necessity","Skin cancers in transplant recipients behave less aggressively than in other patients","Immunosuppression raises the risk of actinic keratosis and skin cancer about sixty-fold","Transplant medication makes the skin more sensitive to burning within minutes of sun"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — transplant recipients need aggressive photoprotection and biannual skin surveillance. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"The parent of a child with a single asymptomatic granuloma annulare ring asks whether treatment is needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is benign and self-limiting, with about half resolving within two years, so treatment is for cosmesis or discomfort","It is premalignant and should be excised to prevent later transformation","It is contagious to other children and school attendance should be limited","It will persist unchanged for life unless systemic immunosuppressive therapy is started early in the course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and there is no malignant potential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with erythema nodosum has no bowel or respiratory symptoms and no recent drug exposure. Which initial investigations should be ordered?","choices":["Complement C4, C1-esterase inhibitor function and serum tryptase","Fasting insulin, haemoglobin A1c and a full lipid profile","Antistreptolysin O titre, throat culture, chest radiograph and inflammatory markers","Serum protein electrophoresis, antinuclear and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with tuberculin or interferon gamma testing and a pregnancy test where relevant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"An infant has generalised blistering from minimal trauma and the subtype is unclear. Which test determines the level of cleavage?","choices":["Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin G","Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for structural antibodies","Bacterial culture of blister fluid with sensitivity testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the gold standard, with immunofluorescence antigen mapping localising the missing protein. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with a body mass index in the 97th percentile has velvety, darkened, thickened skin at the back of the neck and in both axillae. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Fasting glucose, haemoglobin A1c, fasting insulin and a lipid panel","Computed tomography of chest, abdomen and pelvis with upper endoscopy","Potassium hydroxide preparation of scrapings from the affected skin","Skin biopsy of the posterior neck for histological confirmation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the finding is a visible marker of insulin resistance and warrants metabolic assessment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is recovering from carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and is being discharged. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Avoid the causative drug for six months, after which a cautious supervised rechallenge in hospital is reasonable to consider","Expect complete recovery within weeks, with no need for any specialist follow-up after discharge","Avoid sun exposure and use broad-spectrum sunscreen daily to prevent the eruption from recurring","Avoid the causative drug class for life, wear a medical alert bracelet, and have first-degree relatives counselled on shared genetic risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the human leukocyte antigen association makes family counselling part of the discharge advice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has an itchy, eczematous eruption on sun-exposed skin that extends onto covered areas, beginning after two weeks of a new sunscreen. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies","Photopatch testing with duplicate sets, one of which is irradiated","Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B","Skin biopsy of the eruption sent for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the gold standard for photoallergy; a reaction only on the irradiated set confirms it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with painful skin and oral erosions has suspected Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Which diagnostic test is considered pathognomonic?","choices":["Transmission electron microscopy with immunofluorescence antigen mapping","Serum tryptase measured within four hours of the acute eruption","Punch biopsy showing full-thickness epidermal necrosis with junctional separation","Direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A at the papillae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that histology is the pathognomonic finding named for this condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with primary hyperhidrosis says she has been told repeatedly that she just needs to wash more and relax. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Primary hyperhidrosis is a medical condition rather than a problem of hygiene or anxiety","Improved hygiene and stress reduction will resolve the sweating over time","The condition affects a very small minority and treatment options are limited","Sweating of this kind always indicates a serious underlying systemic illness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the misconception is addressed directly, and quality-of-life impairment is comparable to severe psoriasis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with toxic epidermal necrolysis has been admitted to the burn unit and the offending drug stopped. Which pharmacologic therapy has the strongest current evidence base?","choices":["Subcutaneous omalizumab given every four weeks","Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Prophylactic broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics","Pulsed methylprednisolone continued for fourteen days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — early initiation is preferred and the evidence is the strongest of the available options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 12-year-old with recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa attends for review. Which surveillance should now begin?","choices":["Annual bone densitometry to monitor for treatment-related osteoporosis","Annual squamous cell carcinoma surveillance, which starts after the age of ten","Annual colonoscopy to detect gastrointestinal involvement of the disease","Annual echocardiography to monitor for the development of cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the severe scarring of dystrophic disease carries a substantial carcinoma risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with toxic epidermal necrolysis has a SCORTEN of 5 on admission. Beyond intensive care, which referral is specifically indicated at this score?","choices":["Physical therapy, for early prevention of joint contracture","Plastic surgery, for planning of definitive skin grafting","Palliative care, for an early goals-of-care discussion","Genetics, for human leukocyte antigen haplotype testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a score of five or above carries a predicted mortality near 90% and palliative involvement is named. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with rosacea reports gritty, red eyes and blurred vision that has worsened over two weeks. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Advise artificial tears and reassess at his next routine appointment","Urgent ophthalmology referral for slit-lamp examination","Begin topical ivermectin to the face and review in six weeks","Begin sub-antimicrobial doxycycline and review in three months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ocular rosacea with visual symptoms risks corneal scarring if untreated. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with no obesity develops widespread velvety hyperpigmentation over eight weeks, involving the axillae, the oral mucosa and the palms, which have a thickened rugose appearance. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her that the change is cosmetic and arrange routine follow-up with lifestyle advice in six months","Urgent evaluation for an underlying malignancy, particularly of the gastrointestinal tract","Begin metformin to reduce insulin resistance and reassess in three months","Begin topical retinoids for cosmetic improvement of the affected areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — rapid onset, extensive involvement, mucosal change and tripe palms indicate malignant acanthosis nigricans. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
